text:
Excellent Room service, friendly staff
Large room close to pool, refrig, microwave, iron, blowdryer. Great matteress. location close to downtown, the domain, & Round Rock 
 I least like the breakfast... OK, best Non smoking rooms close to the wonderful out door heated pool and Hot tub.:}

Not one, but many, many problems with hotel:
1) Wanting to leave immediately after check-in was impossible because it took 20 minutes to bring bags to room;
2) Room had three hangers for two people staying for three nights;
3) Could not open and close window without calling front desk. It took four hours for someone to come up, preventing us from going to bed;
4) No one knew how to activate Wi-Fi;
5) We were charged for courtesy bar even though we never opened it;
6) On two days housekeeping did not come until we called at 6pm.
We could not believe how bad the service was throughout the hotel. Would never go back!

As other reviews have noted, the Hyatt Hill Country offers a myriad of kid/teen-oriented activities. That, coupled with its close proximity to Sea World, make it a magnet for families. So, if you're considering it for a couple's getaway or a place to stay on business, I recommend that you aim for a time of year when school is in session: we were there for a Monday-Thursday stay the first week in June, and although I anticipated that a weekday stay would help work in favor of a couple's getaway, guests without children were in the distinct minority. Nothing against kids (I have a now-grown one myself), but the public areas (lobby, hallways, elevators, primary restaurant, and grounds just outside the hotel) are thus noisier & less serene.
We sprang for a suite, but unless you crave/need additional space, consider saving yourself the big $$ (we were unable to get the sweet deal on a suite snagged by previous reviewer). Contrary to our expectations, not one of the suites is -- according to the front desk clerk when we asked for another suite after first being assigned one right off the lobby -- an end unit. Rather, they are all at the "joints" of the wings. Although the suites do have a balcony, there is not even a partial wall or slatted railing dividing "your" balcony from your neighbors. Consequently,, ithe balconies end up being another common area offering not even a semblance of privacy... and serve as your neighbors' storage area for water toys, adjunct drying rack for beach towels, or supplemental play area for their children: great for them, but not for a couple hoping to have a quiet conversation & cup of coffee in the AM or a nightcap in the evening.
A couple of less-than-desirable quirks about the interior: 
* there is NO desk, so if you wish to use your laptop, you need to stand at the console housing the mini-fridge; the dining table & chairs are too far away from the modem connection to be a viable alternative to a desk;
* the TV's in both the bedroom and the living room are poorly situated for viewing from the bed or sofa.. best for just 1 person ensconced in the 1 armchair in each of those 2 rooms.; 
* there are no black-out drapes or other means of shielding the bedroom windows: the drapes do not pull all the way across the sheer undercurtains. We are early morning risers, so the sunlight streaming in at dawn didn't bother us, but others might not appreciate that. And, this "feature" too reduces one's privacy;
* for the price we paid, we were expecting, e.g., a more posh bathroom, less skimpy drawer space, and more than just 2 glasses in the suite (i.e., you won't mistake the Hill Country's suite for that of a Four Seasons)
On the up side:
* the lushly landscaped & spacious grounds are a welcome oasis from a day of sightseeing in the humid heat; 
* the adult-only pool & large "hot tub" spa set into a waterfall grotto are lovely & open from 6 AM 'til late at night; 
* we appreciated the free use of bicycles & the easy pool-side availability of beach towels; 
* the golf course gets rave reviews;
* the level of housekeeping & service by the staff was fine, albeit not especially noteworthy
* although you'll pay through the nose to use any of the business services almost always offered for free by other establishments in this (or even lower) price range, there is computer/printer by the concierge desk where you can print off boarding passes at no cost;
* given the distance to downtown San Antonio, the drive there is surprisingly quick & easy; highway congestion does not seem to be common, even during rush hours;
* it was great to be able to so quickly get out into the Hill Country. We had a fun day exploring there, the highlight of our trip imo.
Lastly, I thought the spa was fabulous. It's set off in a separate, very attractive building, somewhat off the beaten path, and has its own, tranquil outdoor pool & large whirlpool with courtyard/patio. The spa space is well thought-out; I received excellent service at prices not out-of-line for this type of facility; the staff was lovely and did not attempt to sell you any products. And yes, men are welcome there too -- plus, the spa offers a light, healthy food throughout the day. In short, couples, singles, and parents looking for a respite from being "on duty" will find this a delightful place to spend time.
I would return to the Hyatt Hill Country, primarily because of the spaciousness & physical beauty of its grounds. But we'll go back with reduced expectations, and not pay the extra money for a suite.

The good:
Friendly staff; speedy check-in and check-out; 24-hour gym;
The bad:
Noisy neighborhood, I've had better in Times Square; tiny bathrooms, I've seen better in some motels and lower classed hotels; no mini bar, never mind not having liquor but no water... The choice was to walk down the hallway and buy water via a vending machine... a vending machine!!! Not at all five star; room service menu was limited, and when I finally ordered something, it took 45 minutes to bring me the wrong item, it was replaced eventually but still....is room service really that busy; I was adjusting my body clock to the local time zone so i was sleeping in late, could not however because of the loud voices by the maid service, laughing and gossiping on the phone and with each other in the hallways;
I choose this hotel thinking it was the Grand Hyatt brand, but I've had better product quality in lower star hotels, service apartments and some motels

Elevators don't work, luggage stuck inside and people were kept outside;
Nothing provided in the room. no water, no toothbrushes;
Staff not friendly at all;
I happened to forget my cell phone chargers in, so i called back, they didn't even want to check it for me, just put me on hold;
won't ever go back again.

This is the 3rd time we stay for 6 days at this property. Large rooms, great location, excelent service, fast internet, easy to access in such a complex city, near nice restaurants, subway. Just 2 remarks;
- Just 1 of the 3 free internet computer on the 12th floor works the right way. Managment should make a small investment changing very old machines
- Will be nice to have a nicer and more welcoming reception/lobby area.

This is neither a luxury hotel or a business hotel. I travel frequently for business and although not all the hotels are luxury, they at least have the type of service required of hotels touting themselves as business hotels. The service on all levels was horrible from the cleaning service to the management, including:
(1) Although I understand that there is no room service due to reservations, when I called Guest Services for recommendations of places that deliver they transferred me to the Front Desk, after being on hold for a good 3 minutes, the Front Desk gave me the number to "The Farm," assuring me that they deliver, they DO NOT DELIVER. When I called and asked them again, they couldn't give me the number to one restaurant/deli that delivered to the hotel;
(2) When boxes I had shipped via FedEx arrived and I asked for them to be delivered to my room, I was told by the Front Desk that they would be right up. They never arrived. I had to call the Front Desk AGAIN several hours later, and ask AGAIN for them to be delivered. No one had any idea what I was talking about;
(3) When I left my room, I saw the cleaning lady for my floor as I had been working in my room all day, I asked her to clean my room since I was leaving for a few hours and gave her the tip directly; my room was NOT CLEAN when I returned and apparently, the cleaning lady's shift had ended;
(4) Finally, I gave them a box to FedEx back to my office on Saturday. I contacted and spoke twice with the Bell Desk who assured me that the box would be sent out on Saturday for Monday morning delivery. The box had the correct label on it, the only thing left to be done was to tape up the box and call FedEx. I have done this at half-a-dozen hotels in the last three months, with no issue. THE BOX WAS NOT HERE ON MONDAY and upon calling the hotel, some other bellman is telling me that the box was shipped out today per my instruction, which is complete BS. However, NO ONE AT FEDEX HAS ANY RECORD OF THE BOX BEING PICKED UP FROM THIS HOTEL ON MONDAY AND NO ONE CURRENTLY KNOWS WHERE THE BOX IS. MANAGEMENT WAS UNHELPFUL AT BEST, DISCOURTEOUS AS A RULE, AND TOOK NO RESPONSIBILITY FOR ITS STAFF. If the Luxe wants to salvage its reputation, the staff at this hotel needs a complete overhaul.

To Whom It May Concern;
I've always enjoyed my stays at The Westin but wanted to recognize Morris Chu for going above and beyond his role to help with a billing question from a few weeks ago. His communication with me has been excellent and has given me the complete confidence my situation would be resolved.
Please let him kmow how much I appreciate his efforts!
